      "text": "Starting with the equation $7^{x+7} = 8^x$, we can rewrite it as $7^7 \\cdot 7^x = 8^x$. \nTo isolate $x$, we can divide both sides by $7^7$ to obtain $7^x = 8^x / 7^7$. \nTaking the natural logarithm of both sides, we get $\\ln(7^x) = \\ln(8^x / 7^7)$. \nUsing the properties of logarithms, we can rewrite this as $x \\ln(7) = \\ln(8) - 7 \\ln(7)$. \nSimplifying, we have $x \\ln(7) = \\ln(8) - 7 \\ln(7)$. \nDividing both sides by $\\ln(7)$, we get $x = \\frac{\\ln(8) - 7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7)}$. \nBy comparing this with the given form $x = \\log_b 7^7$, we see that $b = \\frac{\\ln(8) / \\ln(7)}{7/7} = \\boxed{8}$. \nThe answer is: 8",
